1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:01,312
Three friends.

2
00:00:01,312 --> 00:00:03,117
Three totally different tastes.

3
00:00:03,117 --> 00:00:04,124
One trip.

4
00:00:04,124 --> 00:00:05,618
So who gets their way?

5
00:00:05,618 --> 00:00:07,990
With TasteMatch Trips, everyone does.

6
00:00:08,450 --> 00:00:10,747
Group trips usually get planned for the

7
00:00:10,747 --> 00:00:12,867
majority, or for the loudest friend.

8
00:00:12,867 --> 00:00:15,263
And someone always ends up dragged along.

9
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:16,842
This is the live app.

10
00:00:16,842 --> 00:00:18,420
Each friend adds a nickname

11
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:19,999
and a couple of favourites.

12
00:00:19,999 --> 00:00:22,950
Aziz loves Interstellar and Patagonia.

13
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:25,867
Malika, Daft Punk and Wes Anderson.

14
00:00:25,867 --> 00:00:29,040
Rustam, Anthony Bourdain and Chef's Table.

15
00:00:29,817 --> 00:00:31,852
One click, and the agent goes

16
00:00:31,852 --> 00:00:33,748
to work on Qloo's live API.

17
00:00:33,748 --> 00:00:36,260
Sixteen calls, about four seconds.

18
00:00:37,283 --> 00:00:39,293
Sayulita, Mexico.

19
00:00:39,293 --> 00:00:41,087
Group fit: eighty.

20
00:00:41,087 --> 00:00:43,764
Malika is at ninety percent, and even

21
00:00:43,764 --> 00:00:47,452
the least-matched friend, Rustam, gets seventy-six.

22
00:00:48,150 --> 00:00:50,526
Every city is ranked by fairness: forty

23
00:00:50,526 --> 00:00:53,329
percent average, forty percent the least happy

24
00:00:53,329 --> 00:00:55,461
traveler, twenty percent the group.

25
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:58,911
Then the itinerary: real places, beaches

26
00:00:58,911 --> 00:01:00,775
by day and dinner at night.

27
00:01:00,775 --> 00:01:02,942
Every stop is scored by Qloo for every

28
00:01:02,942 --> 00:01:05,451
traveler, not just the friend who picked it.

29
00:01:05,451 --> 00:01:08,336
This seafood bistro is Rustam's pick, and

30
00:01:08,336 --> 00:01:10,799
Aziz loves it too, at eighty-seven.

31
00:01:11,517 --> 00:01:13,909
Now the agent checks its own work.

32
00:01:13,909 --> 00:01:16,248
The fairness self-check measures how much each

33
00:01:16,248 --> 00:01:18,029
friend should enjoy the whole trip.

34
00:01:18,029 --> 00:01:20,980
Ninety-three, ninety-five, ninety-four.

35
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:23,538
Within two points, so the plan passes.

36
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,018
Every stop sits on one map, and

37
00:01:26,018 --> 00:01:27,900
every Qloo call the agent made is

38
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:29,269
right here in the trace.

39
00:01:30,083 --> 00:01:31,225
Happy with the plan?

40
00:01:31,225 --> 00:01:33,207
Share this plan copies a link, and

41
00:01:33,207 --> 00:01:35,188
Copy for group chat puts the whole

42
00:01:35,188 --> 00:01:37,752
itinerary on your clipboard, ready to paste.

43
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:40,065
Anyone who opens a shared link

44
00:01:40,065 --> 00:01:41,733
gets the group re-planned live.

45
00:01:41,733 --> 00:01:43,666
Here's a harder group, in Paris.

46
00:01:43,666 --> 00:01:45,591
The gap was six points, over the

47
00:01:45,591 --> 00:01:47,937
limit of five, so the agent re-planned.

48
00:01:47,937 --> 00:01:50,749
When that didn't help, it said so honestly.

49
00:01:51,517 --> 00:01:54,210
Under the hood, it's the Qloo Insights API.

50
00:01:54,210 --> 00:01:57,311
Search turns each favourite into a Qloo entity.

51
00:01:57,311 --> 00:01:59,651
Destination insights score cities for the

52
00:01:59,651 --> 00:02:01,933
group, and filter dot entities re-scores

53
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:03,874
the same cities for each traveler.

54
00:02:03,874 --> 00:02:06,961
Place insights fill the itinerary, and filter dot

55
00:02:06,961 --> 00:02:10,112
entities again scores every stop for every friend.

56
00:02:10,112 --> 00:02:13,365
That traveler-by-stop matrix comes from real taste data.

57
00:02:13,365 --> 00:02:15,598
An LLM alone can't produce it.

58
00:02:16,617 --> 00:02:18,025
TasteMatch Trips.

59
00:02:18,025 --> 00:02:20,004
One trip, everyone's taste.

60
00:02:20,004 --> 00:02:21,261
Try it live, and grab

61
00:02:21,261 --> 00:02:23,117
the open-source code on GitHub.
